## 🔄 Singleton Pattern Implementation for Express Server

This PR introduces the **Singleton Pattern** for the Express server setup, ensuring that only one instance of the Express application runs throughout the application lifecycle.

### 📌 **Why use Singleton?**
- Ensures a single shared instance of the Express app.
- Prevents accidental instantiation of multiple servers.
- Promotes better manageability and maintainability of server configurations.

### 🛠 **How it's implemented:**

**`appSingleton.js`**  
This file contains the Singleton class encapsulating the Express setup:

```javascript
import express from "express";
import cors from "cors";
import authRoutes from "./routes/auth.js";

class AppSingleton {
  constructor() {
    if (!AppSingleton.instance) {
      this.app = express();

      // Middleware setup
      this.app.use(cors({
        methods: ["GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE"],
        allowedHeaders: ["Content-Type"]
      }));

      this.app.use(express.json());

      // Routes setup
      this.app.use("/auth", authRoutes);

      AppSingleton.instance = this;
    }
    return AppSingleton.instance;
  }

  getApp() {
    return this.app;
  }
}

const instance = new AppSingleton();
Object.freeze(instance);

export default instance;

